1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,902
A library sorts its books by subject.

2
00:00:02,902 --> 00:00:05,499
A music scale repeats after eight notes.

3
00:00:05,499 --> 00:00:09,605
A shopkeeper keeps an empty shelf
for stock that is still coming.

4
00:00:10,005 --> 00:00:12,750
Sorting, repeating, and leaving a gap.

5
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:15,981
The periodic table was
built on all three ideas.

6
00:00:16,431 --> 00:00:18,396
Aarav sees the problem tonight.

7
00:00:18,396 --> 00:00:22,549
His notes on the elements are a
pile of loose pages, in no order.

8
00:00:22,899 --> 00:00:26,918
Chemists in the eighteen hundreds
had the same mess, only much bigger.

9
00:00:27,418 --> 00:00:31,647
By the end of this video, you will
see how one chemist described elements

10
00:00:31,647 --> 00:00:33,333
that nobody had found yet.

11
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:36,255
So why classify elements at all?

12
00:00:36,605 --> 00:00:40,158
In eighteen hundred, only
thirty one elements were known.

13
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:42,542
By eighteen sixty five, sixty three.

14
00:00:42,892 --> 00:00:45,527
Today we know one hundred and eighteen.

15
00:00:45,527 --> 00:00:48,463
Learning each one alone
is almost impossible.

16
00:00:48,863 --> 00:00:50,989
So we sort them into families.

17
00:00:50,989 --> 00:00:53,417
Elements in one family behave alike.

18
00:00:53,817 --> 00:00:57,855
Learn one member, and you already know
a lot about the rest of its family.

19
00:00:58,505 --> 00:01:01,050
Three big attempts led to the table.

20
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:03,419
Dobereiner in eighteen twenty nine.

21
00:01:03,419 --> 00:01:05,634
Newlands in eighteen sixty five.

22
00:01:05,634 --> 00:01:08,168
And Mendeleev in eighteen sixty nine.

23
00:01:08,818 --> 00:01:11,914
Let's test each idea
on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:11,914 --> 00:01:13,915
First, Dobereiner's triads.

25
00:01:13,915 --> 00:01:16,179
A triad is a group of three.

26
00:01:16,529 --> 00:01:19,076
Calcium, strontium and
barium behave alike.

27
00:01:19,076 --> 00:01:23,457
Their atomic weights are forty, eighty
eight, and one hundred thirty seven.

28
00:01:23,857 --> 00:01:25,763
Add the first and the last.

29
00:01:25,763 --> 00:01:28,914
Forty plus one thirty
seven is one seventy seven.

30
00:01:28,914 --> 00:01:31,569
Half of that is eighty eight point five.

31
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:34,722
And strontium, the middle
one, weighs eighty eight.

32
00:01:34,722 --> 00:01:37,823
The middle weight is close to
the average of the other two.

33
00:01:38,473 --> 00:01:40,517
But only a few triads worked.

34
00:01:40,517 --> 00:01:43,274
So the idea was dropped as a coincidence.

35
00:01:43,674 --> 00:01:44,899
Next, Newlands.

36
00:01:44,899 --> 00:01:48,311
He lined up the elements
in order of atomic weight.

37
00:01:48,711 --> 00:01:53,364
Lithium, beryllium, boron, carbon,
nitrogen, oxygen, fluorine.

38
00:01:53,364 --> 00:01:55,334
Then the eighth is sodium.

39
00:01:55,734 --> 00:01:57,820
And sodium behaves like lithium.

40
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:02,274
Every eighth element is like the
first, just like eight notes on a music

41
00:02:02,274 --> 00:02:03,174
scale.

42
00:02:03,266 --> 00:02:05,312
He called it the law of octaves.

43
00:02:05,312 --> 00:02:07,169
It worked only up to calcium.

44
00:02:07,169 --> 00:02:09,107
After that, the pattern broke.

45
00:02:09,757 --> 00:02:11,129
Then came Mendeleev.

46
00:02:11,129 --> 00:02:15,578
He also ordered the elements by atomic
weight, but he grouped them by how

47
00:02:15,578 --> 00:02:16,508
they behave.

48
00:02:16,908 --> 00:02:18,277
His periodic law.

49
00:02:18,277 --> 00:02:22,843
The properties of the elements are
a periodic function of their atomic

50
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:23,743
weights.

51
00:02:23,602 --> 00:02:26,500
Periodic means repeating at regular steps.

52
00:02:27,150 --> 00:02:29,408
He trusted properties over weights.

53
00:02:29,408 --> 00:02:32,128
Iodine is a little lighter than tellurium.

54
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:35,407
But iodine behaves like
chlorine and bromine.

55
00:02:35,407 --> 00:02:38,700
So he put iodine after
tellurium, in their group.

56
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:42,425
And where no known element
fitted, he left a gap.

57
00:02:42,425 --> 00:02:45,355
A gap below aluminium,
and a gap below silicon.

58
00:02:45,755 --> 00:02:48,343
He even described the missing elements.

59
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:51,355
He called them eka
aluminium and eka silicon.

60
00:02:51,355 --> 00:02:54,267
Eka is Sanskrit for one: one place below.

61
00:02:54,667 --> 00:02:58,992
Look at his guess for eka aluminium,
next to gallium, found in eighteen

62
00:02:58,992 --> 00:02:59,955
seventy five.

63
00:03:00,355 --> 00:03:03,667
Atomic weight, sixty eight
guessed, seventy found.

64
00:03:03,667 --> 00:03:07,698
Density, five point nine guessed,
five point nine four found.

65
00:03:08,098 --> 00:03:10,389
Oxide, E two O three guessed.

66
00:03:10,389 --> 00:03:12,692
Gallium forms G a two O three.

67
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:14,432
Almost a perfect match.

68
00:03:15,082 --> 00:03:16,467
Now eka silicon.

69
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:21,173
It sits right below silicon, and
silicon's oxide is S i O two.

70
00:03:21,573 --> 00:03:22,812
Pause and think.

71
00:03:22,812 --> 00:03:25,239
Which oxide should eka silicon form?

72
00:03:25,239 --> 00:03:27,385
EO two, or E two O three?

73
00:03:30,585 --> 00:03:31,485
EO two!

74
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:34,617
Elements in one group
combine in the same way.

75
00:03:34,617 --> 00:03:38,691
Germanium, found in eighteen
eighty six, forms G e O two.

76
00:03:39,341 --> 00:03:40,422
Remember this.

77
00:03:40,422 --> 00:03:43,167
A good classification does not just sort.

78
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:44,147
It predicts.

79
00:03:44,847 --> 00:03:46,582
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:46,882 --> 00:03:49,651
Chlorine, bromine and iodine form a triad.

81
00:03:49,651 --> 00:03:54,060
Their atomic weights are thirty five
point five, eighty, and one hundred

82
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:55,044
twenty seven.

83
00:03:55,544 --> 00:03:57,713
Does bromine fit Dobereiner's rule?

84
00:03:58,213 --> 00:03:59,113
Step one.

85
00:03:59,066 --> 00:04:01,102
Add the first and the last.

86
00:04:01,102 --> 00:04:06,028
Thirty five point five plus one twenty
seven is one sixty two point five.

87
00:04:06,778 --> 00:04:07,697
Step two.

88
00:04:07,697 --> 00:04:08,673
Take half.

89
00:04:08,673 --> 00:04:13,766
One sixty two point five divided by
two is eighty one point two five.

90
00:04:14,516 --> 00:04:15,416
Step three.

91
00:04:15,378 --> 00:04:16,278
Compare.

92
00:04:16,044 --> 00:04:17,478
Bromine weighs eighty.

93
00:04:17,478 --> 00:04:20,525
That is very close to
eighty one point two five.

94
00:04:21,275 --> 00:04:22,175
So yes.

95
00:04:22,016 --> 00:04:25,935
Bromine sits in the middle,
just as the triad rule says.

96
00:04:26,585 --> 00:04:28,050
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:32,973
Many students write that Mendeleev
arranged the elements by atomic number.

98
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:34,370
He did not.

99
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:36,394
Mendeleev used atomic weights.

100
00:04:36,394 --> 00:04:40,503
Atomic number came later, from
Moseley, in nineteen thirteen.

101
00:04:41,153 --> 00:04:42,697
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:42,697 --> 00:04:44,769
As one mark recall questions.

103
00:04:44,769 --> 00:04:49,109
Triads, octaves, and which real
elements filled Mendeleev's gaps.

104
00:04:49,709 --> 00:04:50,952
Exam level now.

105
00:04:50,952 --> 00:04:54,502
Which statement about early
classification is correct?

106
00:04:54,502 --> 00:04:55,797
Pause and solve.

107
00:05:01,197 --> 00:05:02,615
The answer is C.

108
00:05:02,615 --> 00:05:06,276
Mendeleev's eka aluminium
was later found as gallium.

109
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:07,765
A is wrong.

110
00:05:07,765 --> 00:05:10,830
The law of octaves worked
only up to calcium.

111
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:12,410
B is wrong.

112
00:05:12,410 --> 00:05:16,013
Mendeleev used atomic
weights, not atomic numbers.

113
00:05:16,513 --> 00:05:17,413
D is wrong.

114
00:05:17,369 --> 00:05:21,470
In a triad, the middle weight is
close to the average of the other two,

115
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:22,456
not their sum.

116
00:05:23,056 --> 00:05:24,515
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:24,515 --> 00:05:26,015
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:29,215 --> 00:05:32,052
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:32,452 --> 00:05:33,352
One.

120
00:05:32,970 --> 00:05:36,210
Calcium is forty and
barium is one thirty seven.

121
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:39,339
What weight does the
triad rule give strontium?

122
00:05:40,139 --> 00:05:41,039
Two.

123
00:05:40,675 --> 00:05:44,183
Why did Newlands' law of
octaves fail after calcium?

124
00:05:44,983 --> 00:05:45,883
Three.

125
00:05:45,673 --> 00:05:49,085
Why did Mendeleev place
iodine after tellurium?

126
00:05:49,985 --> 00:05:53,963
Next time: if atomic weight
is not the true key, what is?

127
00:05:53,963 --> 00:05:56,614
We find out in the modern periodic law.
